#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087460 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6381faa4-5dfd-58fa-ace7-411fd2a97e98" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the x-Intercepts of an Ellipse By Completing the Square"}] :question/id #uuid "58d92951-d993-50ec-806c-802c627ada96" :question/math-academy-id "e-5213" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/assets/4527514f6b522bf5922bcba4b77a1574b29f65c238c8b3dd976d6af6bb6c01ae.png)\n\nCalculate the points where the ellipse $x^2 + 3y^2 - 4x -  6y = 2$ (shown above) intercepts the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercepts, we need to substitute $y = 0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-4x-6y & =2 \\\\ {x}^{2}+3\\cdot {0}^{2}-4x-6\\cdot 0 & =2 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x & =2 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-4x+4)-4 & =2 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}-4 & =2 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =6 \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm \\sqrt{6} \\\\ x & =2\\pm \\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at $(2-\\sqrt{6},0)$ and $(2+\\sqrt{6},0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087461 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a508c1-2379-590f-85f5-386a1990f137" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the x-Intercepts of an Ellipse"}] :question/id #uuid "a7fe6697-669c-57e2-8415-4bd40cf76dac" :question/math-academy-id "e-5214" :question/problem "Calculate the point where the ellipse $(x-2)^2 + \\dfrac{(y-2)^2}{4} = 1$ intercepts the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y = 0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+\\frac{(0-2)^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+1 & =1 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x-2 & =0 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at the point $(2,0).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/assets/db1ffc11b3321e87f7c5d2cb490d0381f4b950dfcfd64be00f49c07577f797c0.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087465 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a57ec9ff-99fd-5a3d-9a89-e76ded9d01cb" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the y-Intercepts of an Ellipse"}] :question/id #uuid "85e115e3-4f55-53e6-9d3b-decb3dc02267" :question/math-academy-id "e-5220" :question/problem "Calculate the point where the ellipse $\\dfrac{(x-1)^2}{4}+\\dfrac{(y-3)^2}{16}=1$ intercepts the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x = 0$ in the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $y.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(0-1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ (y-3)^{2} & =12 \\\\ y-3 & =\\pm \\sqrt{12} \\\\ y-3 & =2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ y & =3\\pm 2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at the points $(0,3 -2\\sqrt{3})$ and $(0,3 +2\\sqrt{3}).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/assets/c090d0410cca8d00f0f93641a4f8bfbfa66c3b586894797e5c5c89291d11126d.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087466 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fea426db-a854-5efc-a0cd-7d52019a7cc6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the y-Intercepts of an Ellipse By Completing the Square"}] :question/id #uuid "5cbcffa6-eb3d-5438-ba08-4b47cbbf76ce" :question/math-academy-id "e-5221" :question/problem "Calculate the points where the ellipse $5x^2 + 2y^2 - 20x - 8y + 13 = 0$ intercepts the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $y$-intercepts, we need to substitute $x = 0$ in the equation of the ellipse and try to solve the resulting equation for $y.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-20x-8y+13 & =0 \\\\ 5\\cdot {0}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-20\\cdot 0-8y+13 & =0 \\\\ 2{y}^{2}-8y+13 & =0 \\\\ 2({y}^{2}-4y)+13 & =0 \\\\ 2[{y}^{2}-4y+4-4]+13 & =0 \\\\ 2[(y-2)^{2}-4]+13 & =0 \\\\ 2{(y-2)}^{2}+5 & =0 \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =-\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(y-2)^2 \\geq 0$, there is no $y$ such that $(y-2)^2 = -\\dfrac{5}{2}.$ Therefore, the ellipse has no $y$-intercepts, meaning that it does not intercept the $y$-axis at any point.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/assets/dc56aec7e7c16c4fcfee77f7126f53351c1513c4bfb5e64ac41834ed30ee5782.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267860 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a508c1-2379-590f-85f5-386a1990f137" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the x-Intercepts of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dcf660b4-d4f5-5e3c-bbc1-0920eaadb2d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is no $x$-intercept" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267862} {:answer/id #uuid "7538da33-4499-5a56-8ee1-78740b96168b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267863} {:answer/id #uuid "f715465a-681c-5546-aaf4-b72dcccc54c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267864} {:answer/id #uuid "ef1a01ae-c095-5d05-8219-c89f6c6f83db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267865} {:answer/id #uuid "8d804631-6000-5274-9589-897a4691f407" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267866}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f715465a-681c-5546-aaf4-b72dcccc54c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267864} :answer-field/id #uuid "06d0653b-a7e0-51d3-b91c-7d5c19f29317" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267861}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fca3c3f7-188d-5506-9818-be66c0607803" :question/math-academy-id "q-16642" :question/problem "Calculate the points where the ellipse $\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9}=1$ intercepts the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(0-2)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{4}{9} & =1 \\\\ 9(x-3)^{2}+16 & =36 \\\\ 9(x-3)^{2} & =20 \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =\\frac{20}{9} \\\\ x-3 & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{20}{9}} \\\\ x & =3\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at the points $(3-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3},0)$ and $(3+\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3},0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267867 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a508c1-2379-590f-85f5-386a1990f137" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the x-Intercepts of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01ad77f8-16b1-5bb8-ad29-59b9ef0fc86e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\pm 4,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267869} {:answer/id #uuid "bfb48277-c593-5ee0-8b65-8b6011b2eb4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,0)$ and $(4,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267870} {:answer/id #uuid "e56e4c0e-b228-5a52-8826-1f64bc0761ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is no $x$-intercept" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267871} {:answer/id #uuid "d7b23ee3-7a6f-51f4-98c2-c0dccec79917" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,0)$ and $(4,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267872} {:answer/id #uuid "26a1c010-0e1b-5e5b-bc36-75a8df257575" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\pm 1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267873}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e56e4c0e-b228-5a52-8826-1f64bc0761ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is no $x$-intercept" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267871} :answer-field/id #uuid "b32eca97-6dd3-5ee1-a315-2b1fcbdb1bb5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267868}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "50559ddf-048e-5af7-bb48-9f6c1a546726" :question/math-academy-id "q-16646" :question/problem "Calculate the points if any, where the ellipse $5{x}^{2}+2(y-4)^{2}+20=0$ intercepts the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{2}+2(y-4)^{2} & =-20 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+2(0-4)^{2} & =-20 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+2(16) & =-20 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+32 & =-20 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-\\frac{52}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}^{2}\\ge 0$, there is no $x$ such that ${x}^{2}=-\\frac{52}{5}.$ Therefore, the ellipse has no $x$-intercepts, meaning that it does not intercept the $x$-axis at any point."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267874 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a508c1-2379-590f-85f5-386a1990f137" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the x-Intercepts of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6b7ca58-6523-5b4a-89aa-333c3f91d9ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\pm 3,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267876} {:answer/id #uuid "c4d2ee77-b7e7-5264-8fa6-a323a3d65daf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is no $x$-intercept" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267877} {:answer/id #uuid "e283c48b-6c89-599d-b881-478cdb455ee6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\pm \\sqrt{3},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267878} {:answer/id #uuid "1cb02340-833a-56ca-8205-4cd7a9dbc4e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3\\pm 3\\sqrt{2},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267879} {:answer/id #uuid "d8635ba2-c02d-5b70-adf7-4d27026af194" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267880}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1cb02340-833a-56ca-8205-4cd7a9dbc4e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3\\pm 3\\sqrt{2},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267879} :answer-field/id #uuid "64d96e1d-dc55-5d53-92ab-ea6ad3148922" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267875}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "69c2f5cb-5a68-5c5e-91e4-a196dc268883" :question/math-academy-id "q-16647" :question/problem "Calculate the points where the ellipse $4(x-3)^{2}+9{y}^{2}=72$ intercepts the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x-3)^{2}+9{y}^{2} & =72 \\\\ 4(x-3)^{2}+9\\cdot {0}^{2} & =72 \\\\ 4(x-3)^{2} & =72 \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =18 \\\\ (x-3) & =\\pm \\sqrt{18} \\\\ (x-3) & =\\pm 3\\sqrt{2} \\\\ x & =3\\pm 3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at $(3-3\\sqrt{2},0)$ and $(3+3\\sqrt{2},0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301747 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a57ec9ff-99fd-5a3d-9a89-e76ded9d01cb" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the y-Intercepts of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9117ccb5-342a-51e6-bce2-e863e1c206f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,7\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{7}}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301749} {:answer/id #uuid "668a3a0a-8269-59d2-a600-8840c4fbae70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is no $y$-intercept" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301750} {:answer/id #uuid "067eaf49-7f61-5a27-a4dd-0354b9d1d951" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-7\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301751} {:answer/id #uuid "acc89d54-c83e-5ca2-b01b-119303b680bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301752} {:answer/id #uuid "70f32130-3939-5f94-85c7-a7d384f38596" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\pm 7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301753}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9117ccb5-342a-51e6-bce2-e863e1c206f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,7\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{7}}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301749} :answer-field/id #uuid "aeb0e84c-b7d7-55bc-b40c-174c62dc7ac0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301748}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "73127ff2-1cda-57f0-ae1f-5bfe9c540720" :question/math-academy-id "q-16683" :question/problem "Calculate the points where the ellipse $\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y-7)^{2}}{9}=1$ intercepts the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $y.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y-7)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(0+3)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y-7)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{9}{16}+\\frac{(y-7)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ 81+16(y-7)^{2} & =9\\cdot 16 \\\\ 81+16(y-7)^{2} & =144 \\\\ 16(y-7)^{2} & =63 \\\\ (y-7)^{2} & =\\frac{63}{16} \\\\ y-7 & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{63}{16}} \\\\ y-7 & =\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{7}}{4} \\\\ y & =7\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{7}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at the points $(0,7-\\frac{3\\sqrt{7}}{4})$ and $(0,7+\\frac{3\\sqrt{7}}{4}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087986 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a57ec9ff-99fd-5a3d-9a89-e76ded9d01cb" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the y-Intercepts of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b78e586b-3438-5a1e-a4ae-50961f688263" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is no $y$-intercept" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084928} {:answer/id #uuid "cfba57e9-bd04-52c1-9b29-5037ee6071fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-6)$ and $(0, 3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084929} {:answer/id #uuid "2d78f733-7eb1-5f91-b23d-3418fdfe6c0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-3)$ and $(0, 6)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084930} {:answer/id #uuid "45e5e10e-e975-5e02-aecb-d9121e0d9255" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084931} {:answer/id #uuid "64d2205b-88c5-5869-8dc8-396f016aebd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084932}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45e5e10e-e975-5e02-aecb-d9121e0d9255" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084931} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c107f19-9d29-5977-bda9-8e61c508aa57" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084927}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0590ee5b-b1c4-50b4-ad4a-94aa0c689fb0" :question/math-academy-id "q-16684" :question/problem "Determine the points where the ellipse $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{36}=1$ intercepts the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $y.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{36} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(0-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{36} & =1 \\\\ 1+\\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{36} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{36} & =0 \\\\ y+6 & =0 \\\\ y & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,-6).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/assets/d3780834bd9f303ee9d7a6fff0c1fcf28ccfff4cbe4d69c9ec38691a33aca7e1.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301754 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fea426db-a854-5efc-a0cd-7d52019a7cc6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the y-Intercepts of an Ellipse By Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34a2a44e-9d5d-5571-908f-6903bddf08fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,2\\pm \\sqrt{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301756} {:answer/id #uuid "06474273-cf38-5bae-9e83-195d631e102b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-2\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{5}{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301757} {:answer/id #uuid "6d7f3fd4-593c-541b-8090-68c17147f6cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,1\\pm \\sqrt{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301758} {:answer/id #uuid "5125ae59-7059-51a8-946c-db543914673a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-1\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301759} {:answer/id #uuid "ba60c560-9fc8-5aff-b3c4-41b118b1a8e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-5\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301760}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06474273-cf38-5bae-9e83-195d631e102b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-2\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{5}{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301757} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a7533d1-057b-5731-83ed-97f026bf7179" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301755}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7ac2dbe7-d30d-532d-9aae-ed5a7b744bef" :question/math-academy-id "q-170551" :question/problem "Determine the points where the ellipse $3{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-12x+8y+3=0$ intercepts the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $y$-intercepts, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and try to solve the resulting equation for $y.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-12x+8y+3 & =0 \\\\ 3(0)^{2}+2{y}^{2}-12(0)+8y+3 & =0 \\\\ 2{y}^{2}+8y+3 & =0 \\\\ 2[{y}^{2}+4y]+3 & =0 \\\\ 2[(y+2)^{2}-4]+3 & =0 \\\\ 2(y+2)^{2}-8+3 & =0 \\\\ 2(y+2)^{2} & =5 \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ y+2 & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{5}{2}} \\\\ y & =-2\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{5}{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,-2-\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{2}})$ and $(0,-2+\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{2}}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301761 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6381faa4-5dfd-58fa-ace7-411fd2a97e98" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the x-Intercepts of an Ellipse By Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91fcaded-11a6-5d75-93a2-c82926af1fe6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5-\\sqrt{35}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301763}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91fcaded-11a6-5d75-93a2-c82926af1fe6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5-\\sqrt{35}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301763} :answer-field/id #uuid "1be17426-dedb-5659-8abd-74cac156ddf0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301762} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eef22395-cf70-585c-b636-9b76eb7bcf15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301765}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eef22395-cf70-585c-b636-9b76eb7bcf15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301765} :answer-field/id #uuid "2082b768-07b8-5fe8-9073-662787509de8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301764} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af3970bc-9d72-5584-b03c-a767f192ddb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5+\\sqrt{35}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301767}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af3970bc-9d72-5584-b03c-a767f192ddb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5+\\sqrt{35}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301767} :answer-field/id #uuid "5bb6ea7c-3b0b-5a33-9d6c-9a029f7e16d5" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301766} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "904bf4fd-ddba-56d5-bdf1-fcf514fb7c6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301769}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "904bf4fd-ddba-56d5-bdf1-fcf514fb7c6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301769} :answer-field/id #uuid "658d3350-05bf-5df7-8277-17ef8c8eac2e" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301768}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "05ce5300-b428-5c46-807f-7bcb234ad471" :question/math-academy-id "q-274480" :question/problem "From **left to right**, the ellipse $5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+50x-4y=50$ intercepts the $x$-axis at the points $A$ and $B.$ Find the coordinates of these points.\n\n$A($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}$)\\,$\n\nand \n\n$\\,B($\n{{field-3}}$,$\n{{field-4}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercepts, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+50x-4y & =50 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+(0)^{2}+50x-4(0) & =50 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+50x & =50 \\\\ {x}^{2}+10x & =10 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+10x+25)-25 & =10 \\\\ (x+5)^{2}-25 & =10 \\\\ (x+5)^{2} & =35 \\\\ x+5 & =\\pm \\sqrt{35} \\\\ x & =-5\\pm \\sqrt{35}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the $x$-intercepts are\n\n$$\nA(-5-\\sqrt{35},0)\\,\\text{and}\\,B(-5+\\sqrt{35},0).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301770 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6381faa4-5dfd-58fa-ace7-411fd2a97e98" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the x-Intercepts of an Ellipse By Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b3b7519-12eb-5ab1-9892-4ad1ef482105" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301772}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b3b7519-12eb-5ab1-9892-4ad1ef482105" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301772} :answer-field/id #uuid "4685700b-2f4c-5738-8854-51b4e36bcdf7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301771} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "afb77033-5bff-5f7b-af40-2852be4204b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301774}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "afb77033-5bff-5f7b-af40-2852be4204b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301774} :answer-field/id #uuid "25f10560-6332-57b2-8efb-723c4109450c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301773}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "55778a1f-c4b7-5bf3-8c36-14a56c602b7c" :question/math-academy-id "q-326042" :question/problem "Calculate the point where the ellipse  $4{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}+12x+24y+9=0$ intercepts the $x$-axis.\n\nThe $x$-intercept is $($\n{{field-1}}\n,\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercepts, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}+12x+24y+9 & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+6\\cdot {0}^{2}+12x+24\\cdot 0+9 & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+12x+9 & =0 \\\\ (2x+3)^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2x+3 & =0 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at  $(-\\frac{3}{2},0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267931 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fea426db-a854-5efc-a0cd-7d52019a7cc6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the y-Intercepts of an Ellipse By Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce037c27-1191-59ab-91c7-ce7d84ebb5c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267933} {:answer/id #uuid "74edc858-86e8-5410-81a1-f3add125c5ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301775}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce037c27-1191-59ab-91c7-ce7d84ebb5c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267933} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7e89b1e-0fa0-5146-819c-88d513ab13c2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267932} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b790113-deef-5aab-be8b-e1d9d281a34d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267935}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b790113-deef-5aab-be8b-e1d9d281a34d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267935} :answer-field/id #uuid "782fc26c-27bb-5025-9bdd-006f8586f5cb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267934}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "167fbd42-673a-5411-ba0f-804405134964" :question/math-academy-id "q-327146" :question/problem "Calculate the point where the ellipse $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8x-5y+\\frac{25}{4}=0$ intercepts the $y$-axis.\n\nThe $y$-intercept is $($\n{{field-1}}\n,\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and try to solve the resulting equation for $y.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8x-5y+\\frac{25}{4} & =0 \\\\ 4(0)^{2}+{y}^{2}-8(0)-5y+\\frac{25}{4} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-5y+\\frac{25}{4} & =0 \\\\ {(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ y-\\frac{5}{2} & =0 \\\\ y & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,\\frac{5}{2}).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/assets/0a1303644a54b07f874df4f6694d84384e634403bc4efe8ee5086d922bc6f25e.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301776 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6381faa4-5dfd-58fa-ace7-411fd2a97e98" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the x-Intercepts of an Ellipse By Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d2e8bef-3a84-5830-8166-839fd04e61d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301778} {:answer/id #uuid "44c19436-d41e-5cdc-a48e-4292fb07e975" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301779}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d2e8bef-3a84-5830-8166-839fd04e61d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301778} :answer-field/id #uuid "83df15e2-e567-5a33-8571-27d624ee56a8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301777} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d46f894a-4f2a-5937-b897-37f9e01b0c50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301781}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d46f894a-4f2a-5937-b897-37f9e01b0c50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301781} :answer-field/id #uuid "01b76107-9684-58ec-b4b2-9a9a53b52888" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301780} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ec7dd95-2852-5c7e-a6ab-fe5d775e0f41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301783}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ec7dd95-2852-5c7e-a6ab-fe5d775e0f41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301783} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2db51bc-7ec9-514f-955c-168df0196329" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301782} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2afd706d-3078-5d97-9588-aa5e7c2eead3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301785}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2afd706d-3078-5d97-9588-aa5e7c2eead3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301785} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ce9d939-eba9-5928-8276-0a468a38c516" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301784}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b832e87a-3420-551d-b671-3aff33c05a11" :question/math-academy-id "q-328497" :question/problem "From **left to right**, the ellipse ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+12x+4y=28$ intercepts the $x$-axis at the points $A$ and $B.$ Find the coordinates of these points.\n\n$A($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}$)\\,$\n\nand \n\n$\\,B($\n{{field-3}}$,$\n{{field-4}}$)\\,$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercepts, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+12x+4y & =28 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(0)^{2}+12x+4(0) & =28 \\\\ {x}^{2}+12x & =28 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+12x+36)-36 & =28 \\\\ (x+6)^{2}-36 & =28 \\\\ (x+6)^{2} & =64 \\\\ x+6 & =\\pm 8 \\\\ x & =-6\\pm 8 \\\\ x & =-14,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the $x$-intercepts are\n\n$$\nA(-14,0)\\,\\text{and}\\,B(2,0).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301786 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fea426db-a854-5efc-a0cd-7d52019a7cc6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the y-Intercepts of an Ellipse By Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "271d39ba-e44c-5311-a27e-98f4bbbe3deb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301788}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "271d39ba-e44c-5311-a27e-98f4bbbe3deb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301788} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab9005b6-00df-50d2-8547-02da027e4dab" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301787} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8e386c0-6a07-5878-8816-3d60c53aaaf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4+\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301790}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8e386c0-6a07-5878-8816-3d60c53aaaf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4+\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301790} :answer-field/id #uuid "864d7b14-dde6-5d80-a7bc-06a70ca7f3b8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301789} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c831e31-dd54-5db2-9e74-0b0993ccafd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301792}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c831e31-dd54-5db2-9e74-0b0993ccafd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301792} :answer-field/id #uuid "14792e58-8eca-523e-9254-54ffdf953629" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301791} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9aa6185-f223-5c74-b2f6-302f995f077f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4-\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301794}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9aa6185-f223-5c74-b2f6-302f995f077f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4-\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301794} :answer-field/id #uuid "f14471b2-f68b-5fec-a75d-cc3a3b46c24e" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301793}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "297fe128-9264-50ed-838b-4c61ab2d1cbc" :question/math-academy-id "q-328517" :question/problem "From **top to bottom**, the ellipse $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8x-8y+11=0$ intercepts the $y$-axis at the points $A$ and $B.$ Find the coordinates of these points.\n\n$A($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}$)\\,$\n\nand \n\n$\\,B($\n{{field-3}}$,$\n{{field-4}}$)\\,$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $y$-intercepts, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and try to solve the resulting equation for $y.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8x-8y+11 & =0 \\\\ 4(0)^{2}+{y}^{2}-8\\cdot 0-8y+11 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-8y+11 & =0 \\\\ [{y}^{2}-8y]+11 & =0 \\\\ [(y-4)^{2}-16]+11 & =0 \\\\ (y-4)^{2}-5 & =0 \\\\ (y-4)^{2} & =5 \\\\ y-4 & =\\pm \\sqrt{5} \\\\ y & =4\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom, the $y$-intercepts are\n\n$$\nA\\,(0,4+\\sqrt{5})\\,\\text{and}\\,B\\,(0,4-\\sqrt{5}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301795 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a57ec9ff-99fd-5a3d-9a89-e76ded9d01cb" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the y-Intercepts of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d0fb522-af20-50be-8320-72979132453e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301797}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d0fb522-af20-50be-8320-72979132453e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301797} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2f3adc0-d12b-5784-8b8b-56889690c76c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301796} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b2d58e0-d1f6-500c-bc5b-f51f57ddeaf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301799}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b2d58e0-d1f6-500c-bc5b-f51f57ddeaf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301799} :answer-field/id #uuid "6148b6ee-79a6-592d-a4cc-8b5465cc84ab" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301798} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b42ec80-7bc2-53a8-bd9f-3372bfe2fb84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301801}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b42ec80-7bc2-53a8-bd9f-3372bfe2fb84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301801} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f84eea5-2fb7-5c5b-9eae-7b0dcf4327fd" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301800}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "089dcf31-aaaf-55cc-ada0-5bd4d18c4900" :question/math-academy-id "q-328525" :question/problem "From **top to bottom**, the ellipse $\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{12}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{9}=1$ intercepts the $y$-axis at the points $A$ and $B.$ Find the coordinates of these points.\n\n$A($\n{{field-1}}\n$,-\\frac{1}{2})\\,$\n\nand \n\n$\\,B($\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $y.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{12}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(0-3)^{2}}{12}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{9}{12}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ 9\\,(\\frac{3}{4})+(y+2)^{2} & =9 \\\\ \\frac{27}{4}+(y+2)^{2} & =\\frac{36}{4} \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ y+2 & =\\pm \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y & =-2\\pm \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{7}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom, the $y$-intercepts are\n\n$$\nA(0,\\,-\\frac{1}{2})\\,\\text{and}\\,B(0,\\,-\\frac{7}{2}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301802 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a57ec9ff-99fd-5a3d-9a89-e76ded9d01cb" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the y-Intercepts of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b200b06-bf5f-533c-bfdf-84942b8d6f80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301804}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b200b06-bf5f-533c-bfdf-84942b8d6f80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301804} :answer-field/id #uuid "6983fe3a-713b-5b13-8875-055cf9b0717e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301803} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef63e84b-4e83-5206-a522-6c11382c87a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301806}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef63e84b-4e83-5206-a522-6c11382c87a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301806} :answer-field/id #uuid "d93b1327-0537-5563-84b9-4738a20bc1a4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301805} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ea3d861-8a74-54fa-9573-6809f894919d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{22}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301808}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ea3d861-8a74-54fa-9573-6809f894919d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{22}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301808} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6ea4876-62c4-5fb1-9e27-9ab0febf1402" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301807}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a9b9dc65-fcd0-5105-8764-5a33b3bbe450" :question/math-academy-id "q-328527" :question/problem "From **top to bottom**, the ellipse $\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{16}=1$ intercepts the $y$-axis at the points $A$ and $B.$ Find the coordinates of these points.\n\n$A($\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\frac{2}{5})\\,$\n\nand \n\n$\\,B($\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $y.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(0-4)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{16}{25}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ 256+25(y+2)^{2} & =400 \\\\ 25(y+2)^{2} & =144 \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =\\frac{144}{25} \\\\ y+2 & =\\pm \\frac{12}{5} \\\\ y & =-2\\pm \\frac{12}{5} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{22}{5},\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom, the $y$-intercepts are\n\n$$\nA(0,\\frac{2}{5})\\,\\text{and}\\,B(0,-\\frac{22}{5}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267942 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6381faa4-5dfd-58fa-ace7-411fd2a97e98" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the x-Intercepts of an Ellipse By Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e85a367-2302-57e9-962b-f328530e6515" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\pm \\sqrt{3},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267944} {:answer/id #uuid "48c2c761-77e4-540e-b74a-7c7343e36a22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is no $x$-intercept" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267945} {:answer/id #uuid "4c01566b-b810-5053-86db-734c63ece004" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2\\pm \\sqrt{3},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267946} {:answer/id #uuid "e967b97f-698b-5bc0-9412-c86232ffb994" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267947} {:answer/id #uuid "8aa028fc-f755-56af-8dd4-05d189f371f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267948}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e85a367-2302-57e9-962b-f328530e6515" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\pm \\sqrt{3},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267944} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c7a00b5-b64e-53d2-b2a0-a158b06a42ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267943}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "64b3860f-fd90-5411-a2e5-411bcddf0dbc" :question/math-academy-id "q-72914" :question/problem "Calculate the points where the ellipse ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4x-2y+1=0$ intercepts the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercepts, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4x-2y+1 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{0}^{2}-4x-2(0)+1 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+1 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}-4+1 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =3 \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm \\sqrt{3} \\\\ x & =2\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at $(2-\\sqrt{3},0)$ and $(2+\\sqrt{3},0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301809 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a508c1-2379-590f-85f5-386a1990f137" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the x-Intercepts of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c764b04-3a63-50dc-8649-e02947fb4529" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\pm 3,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301811} {:answer/id #uuid "cf71a89d-1726-5d5d-bbe6-361d4d0c204c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\pm 2,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301812} {:answer/id #uuid "d7a44dac-9d5e-5737-9bb2-7bcb3d97c969" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ and $(4,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301813} {:answer/id #uuid "925d5be1-7baf-5e6f-834e-8e2020b32b91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ and $(2,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301814} {:answer/id #uuid "3370a621-b371-5374-818a-188677faea74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,0)$ and $(2,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301815}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7a44dac-9d5e-5737-9bb2-7bcb3d97c969" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ and $(4,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301813} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1da93ea-39a3-520c-82fb-4ee03a9f2bbb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301810}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "85a05273-d7c5-5a81-9a38-af7c6efc1cfa" :question/math-academy-id "q-7643" :question/problem "Calculate the points where the ellipse \n$\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{12}=1$ intercepts the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{12} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(0+3)^{2}}{12} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{9}{12} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{3}{4} & =1 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+12 & =16 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ x & =2\\pm 2 \\\\ x & =0,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088398 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a508c1-2379-590f-85f5-386a1990f137" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the x-Intercepts of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cb3c0df-3832-577b-827a-26d8e4d65bbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-5, 0)$ and $(-3, 0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087319} {:answer/id #uuid "e3a6f6ab-b141-5abe-bc31-f1709de323bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3, 0)$ and $(5, 0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087320} {:answer/id #uuid "0481e7c3-6950-5fe0-8d3d-2ea93680d586" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-4, 0)$ and $(-2, 0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087321} {:answer/id #uuid "398d82c9-4cc1-547d-9f52-d8b2e613f1ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2, 0)$ and $(2, 0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087322} {:answer/id #uuid "53af3dde-ce2b-5aee-a79c-d7d102c042de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2, 0)$ and $(4, 0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087323}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9cb3c0df-3832-577b-827a-26d8e4d65bbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-5, 0)$ and $(-3, 0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087319} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d6b1db7-08c4-56d0-a6bb-ce1468649e28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087318}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8fc78834-5b61-5384-b707-af7562e6a703" :question/math-academy-id "q-9457" :question/problem "Calculate the points where the ellipse $\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{6}=1$ intercepts the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{6} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(0+2)^{2}}{6} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{4}{6} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{2}{3} & =1 \\\\ (x+4)^{2}+2 & =3 \\\\ (x+4)^{2} & =1 \\\\ x+4 & =\\pm 1 \\\\ x & =-4\\pm 1 \\\\ x & =-5,\\,-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at the points $(-5,0)$ and $(-3,0).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/assets/f491b886477531fbd081cbdd73244e9cf489bd2474528ea27b66b187b9eb170e.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301816 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6381faa4-5dfd-58fa-ace7-411fd2a97e98" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the x-Intercepts of an Ellipse By Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc13af03-e5f2-54f9-a17c-a6649aae82a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3\\pm 2\\sqrt{3},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301818} {:answer/id #uuid "0d0f82d5-1461-56ce-88c6-ffc4901b6b08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\pm \\sqrt{3},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301819} {:answer/id #uuid "01e87915-1e7b-562c-8c11-a63d42719a98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-3,0)$ and $(1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301820} {:answer/id #uuid "053cac49-342e-57f5-998c-bba4681483a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-5,0)$ and $(2,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301821} {:answer/id #uuid "5dad6ca9-58c7-59eb-8853-5ff946f23abe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301822}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc13af03-e5f2-54f9-a17c-a6649aae82a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3\\pm 2\\sqrt{3},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301818} :answer-field/id #uuid "14a15f87-933a-5b64-9f43-2de5fd23f841" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301817}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c118445f-8af4-5515-9f4b-7c6040808075" :question/math-academy-id "q-9461" :question/problem "Calculate the points where the ellipse ${x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}+6x+6y=3$  intercepts the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercepts, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}+6x+6y & =3 \\\\ {x}^{2}+3\\cdot {0}^{2}+6x+6\\cdot 0 & =3 \\\\ {x}^{2}+6x & =3 \\\\ (x+3)^{2}-9 & =3 \\\\ (x+3)^{2} & =3+9 \\\\ (x+3)^{2} & =12 \\\\ x+3 & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ x & =-3\\pm 2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at $(-3-2\\sqrt{3},0)$ and $(-3+2\\sqrt{3},0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301823 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a57ec9ff-99fd-5a3d-9a89-e76ded9d01cb" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the y-Intercepts of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d9805dc2-05da-5fff-ba9e-2236db0cbb39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301825} {:answer/id #uuid "553f9051-6c24-5eee-8280-8bdfafa595da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301826} {:answer/id #uuid "a58cb241-c60e-5299-ad58-a5a5359e57fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is no $y$-intercept" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301827} {:answer/id #uuid "b6ce7357-8e94-55f0-8254-f92c8ed12a07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-3)$ and $(0,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301828} {:answer/id #uuid "5d156dea-6432-572c-a444-30a17b7705c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-2)$ and $(0,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301829}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a58cb241-c60e-5299-ad58-a5a5359e57fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is no $y$-intercept" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301827} :answer-field/id #uuid "6be86e06-edb0-5a45-8312-a3372faf7842" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301824}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "abc24ff4-9a14-532c-930a-11e5cec7f6a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-9467" :question/problem "Calculate the points where the ellipse $\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}=1$ intercepts the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $y.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{(x+2)}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{(y-3)}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{(0+2)}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{(y-3)}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{{(y-3)}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ 12+{(y-3)}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {(y-3)}^{2} & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(y-3)^{2}\\ge 0$, there is no $y$ such that $(y-3)^{2}=-3.$ Therefore, the ellipse has no $y$-intercepts, meaning that it does not intercept the $y$-axis at any point."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301830 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fea426db-a854-5efc-a0cd-7d52019a7cc6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the y-Intercepts of an Ellipse By Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b5cf51a-0bc3-58a6-bf67-dea1cafc39fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-3)$ and $(0,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301832} {:answer/id #uuid "c3ec3f05-236c-5e61-a849-f454c4a51fc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-1)$ and $(0,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301833} {:answer/id #uuid "197fc32b-18f8-5f1d-b7bb-b4ddc9945fc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is no $y$-intercept" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301834} {:answer/id #uuid "051e7c67-9edb-56a3-90f4-5a88147c07d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301835} {:answer/id #uuid "c4897e7d-684d-5124-b228-fe3dd22b31f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301836}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "197fc32b-18f8-5f1d-b7bb-b4ddc9945fc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is no $y$-intercept" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301834} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e17f1e2-fdd5-5bd6-b6a3-fe3360f784c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301831}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9f068945-a72d-5d56-b777-f84b01c8b6a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-9468" :question/problem "Determine the point where the ellipse ${x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}-8x+24y+28=0$ intercepts the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $y$-intercepts, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and try to solve the resulting equation for $y.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}-8x+24y+28 & =0 \\\\ {0}^{2}+6{y}^{2}-8\\cdot 0+24y+28 & =0 \\\\ 6{y}^{2}+24y+28 & =0 \\\\ 6[{y}^{2}+4y]+28 & =0 \\\\ 6[(y+2)^{2}-4]+28 & =0 \\\\ 6(y+2)^{2}-24+28 & =0 \\\\ 6(y+2)^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ 6(y+2)^{2} & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $6(y+2)^{2}\\ge 0$, there is no $y$ such that $6(y+2)^{2}=-4.$ Therefore, the ellipse has no $y$-intercepts, meaning that it does not intercept the $y$-axis at any point."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088400 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fea426db-a854-5efc-a0cd-7d52019a7cc6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the y-Intercepts of an Ellipse By Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9abf29ce-8294-543f-b99d-8a0d65470c60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, \\sqrt{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087331} {:answer/id #uuid "9bc3b2e7-84cc-5cf9-996d-3449019f883a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, 1 + \\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087332} {:answer/id #uuid "a80996d7-606b-55d7-86be-28a64364c5b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087333} {:answer/id #uuid "b78ccb87-3284-5860-8323-91ddc859c15d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087334} {:answer/id #uuid "53388488-be45-57cb-9bb3-33c6f6501b30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, 1 + \\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087335}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9abf29ce-8294-543f-b99d-8a0d65470c60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, \\sqrt{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087331} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ccd0095-de07-5739-8b9d-7dc31eaa5b8e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087330}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0d07a4b9-9421-5c45-b2ed-394d687e3601" :question/math-academy-id "q-9471" :question/problem "Determine the point where the ellipse ${x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-4\\sqrt{3}x-6\\sqrt{5}y+15=0$ intercepts the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $y$-intercepts, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and try to solve the resulting equation for $y.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-4\\sqrt{3}x-6\\sqrt{5}y+15 & =0 \\\\ {0}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-4\\sqrt{3}(0)-6\\sqrt{5}y+15 & =0 \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-6\\sqrt{5}y+15 & =0 \\\\ 3[{y}^{2}-2\\sqrt{5}y]+15 & =0 \\\\ 3[(y-\\sqrt{5})^{2}-5]+15 & =0 \\\\ 3(y-\\sqrt{5})^{2}-15+15 & =0 \\\\ 3(y-\\sqrt{5})^{2} & =0 \\\\ y-\\sqrt{5} & =0 \\\\ y & =\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,\\sqrt{5}).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/assets/d37e546bf2b17c99c87650e7c0973a3a881c8c5f3a8ed28f5f923d9d3d500511.png)"}]}
